    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p131-q44-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $h(-x)=f(x)+g(x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$h(-x)=f(-x)+g(-x)=f(x)+g(x)$, since both functions are even.",
          "worked": "On the common domain, $f(-x)=f(x)$ and $g(-x)=g(x)$ because both functions are even.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}h(-x)&=f(-x)+g(-x)\\\\h(-x)&=f(x)+g(x).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p131-q44-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, what kind of function is $h(x)$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Even, since $h(-x)=h(x)$ on the common domain.",
          "worked": "Using part (a) and the definition of $h$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}h(-x)&=f(x)+g(x)\\\\h(-x)&=h(x).\\end{align*}$$\n\nTherefore, $h$ is even on the common domain of $f$ and $g$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p131-q44-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "What general conclusion can be made from this?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The sum of two even functions is even on their common domain.",
          "worked": "The argument applies to any two even functions: their sum satisfies $h(-x)=h(x)$ wherever both functions are defined. Thus the sum of two even functions is even on their common domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      }
    ]
  },
